            "type": "p",
            "html": "The ideological struggle also became existential with the <span class=\"kt\">nuclear arms race</span>. The United States had used atomic bombs in 1945. The Soviet Union tested its first atomic bomb in 1949, years earlier than U.S. intelligence had predicted. Within a decade, both sides had developed hydrogen bombs hundreds of times more powerful. By the late 1950s, both possessed enough warheads to destroy civilization. This produced the doctrine of <span class=\"kt\">Mutually Assured Destruction (MAD)</span>: both sides understood that a nuclear first strike would trigger a response that would annihilate both. The Cold War stayed cold, but only barely."
