            "type": "p",
            "html": "Not everyone accepted the Cold War's binary. In April 1955, representatives of 29 Asian and African nations gathered in Bandung, Indonesia, for a conference that had no superpower host and no superpower agenda. They came from countries that had recently won independence from European empires, or were still fighting for it. Their shared experience was not of capitalism versus communism. It was of colonialism: the experience of being governed, exploited, and dismissed by foreign powers for generations."
