    "takeaway": "The 20th century produced three broad categories of reaction to existing power structures: groups and individuals who promoted nonviolence as a path to political change (Gandhi, King, Mandela, KC-6.2.V.A); militarized states whose responses to conflict intensified it rather than resolved it (Pinochet, Franco, Amin, the military–industrial complex, KC-6.2.V.C); and non-state movements that targeted civilians as an instrument of political strategy (Shining Path, Al-Qaeda, KC-6.2.V.D). The AP skill is explaining what distinguishes these categories, not what they share, but how and why each represents a different theory of how political change happens, or doesn't.",
