            "type": "p",
            "html": "<span class=\"kt\">U.S. intervention in Latin America</span> (Topics 8.3, 8.7) produced the Western Hemisphere's clearest political and social Cold War effects. The CIA-backed coup in Guatemala (1954) overthrew a democratically elected government whose land reform program was labeled communist. Pinochet's 1973 coup in Chile, with U.S. support, ended Latin America's longest democratic tradition and installed a military government that killed, tortured, and disappeared thousands. Nicaragua, El Salvador, and Honduras became theaters for U.S. proxy operations in the 1980s. These were Cold War political and social effects in the Western Hemisphere, just operating through U.S. power rather than Soviet power."
